Modern Fortran — Developer Quick
Reference
A compact orientation sheet for developers working with modern Fortran. Check compiler and project
documentation for version-specific behavior.
Core source structure
program main
implicit none
print *, 'Hello, Fortran!'
end program main
Useful language habits
• Use implicit none in program units to avoid unintended implicit typing.
• Prefer iso_fortran_env kinds such as real64 when a specific precision is required.
• Use modules to organize reusable procedures, derived types and shared interfaces.
• Use allocatable for dynamically sized data when ownership and lifetime are clear.
• Check array bounds, intent attributes and I/O status in robust code.
Build and package workflow
Use the compiler documentation for your selected compiler. For package-oriented projects, explore fpm (Fortran
Package Manager) and its official documentation. Keep build instructions reproducible and record compiler
versions.
Language server workflow
Install fortls from its official upstream instructions, configure your editor's Language Server Protocol client, and
check the fortls repository for current configuration options. The FORTLS browser helper checks JSON syntax
only; it is not a semantic validator.
Official references
fortls: https://github.com/fortran-lang/fortls
Fortran community: https://fortran-lang.org/
fpm: https://fpm.fortran-lang.org/
GNU Fortran: https://gcc.gnu.org/fortran/
FORTLS.com is an independent resource hub and is not the official fortls project.